Diagnostic for ambiguous Delaunay triangulations in 3D mortar integration (not for merging).
Reports every clip polygon vertex that lies within a relative distance of 1e-6 of the
circumcircle of a triangle, and on which side round-off put it; see the comments in the code below.

Usage on main:
  git apply delaunay_ambiguous_diagnostic.patch
  (build, run a test, search its output for "AMBIGUOUS DELAUNAY TRIANGULATION")
  git checkout src/mortar/src/4C_mortar_coupling3d.cpp

--- a/src/mortar/src/4C_mortar_coupling3d.cpp
+++ b/src/mortar/src/4C_mortar_coupling3d.cpp
@@ -2945,6 +2945,27 @@
         const double diff = abs(dist - radius1);
         if (diff < close[t]) close[t] = diff;

+        //**********************************************************************
+        // DIAGNOSTIC (not for merging): detect ambiguous Delaunay triangulations
+        //
+        // If vertex k lies (almost) on the circumcircle of triangle t, both diagonals of the
+        // quadrilateral formed by them are valid Delaunay triangulations, and the decision below
+        // (dist < radius1, without tolerance) is made by round-off. The relative tolerance is the
+        // one used by the fix (MORTARDELAUNAYTOL). Comparing the reported decision between Newton
+        // iterations for the same element pair shows whether the triangulation flips.
+        //**********************************************************************
+        {
+          const double relative_tolerance = 1.0e-6;
+          if (diff < relative_tolerance * radius1)
+            std::cout << "AMBIGUOUS DELAUNAY TRIANGULATION: source element "
+                      << source_element().id() << ", target element " << target_element().id()
+                      << ", polygon with " << clipsize << " vertices: vertex " << k
+                      << " is at a relative distance of " << (dist - radius1) / radius1
+                      << " from the circumcircle of triangle (" << idx0 << ", " << idx1 << ", "
+                      << idx2 << "), decided " << (dist < radius1 ? "inside" : "outside")
+                      << std::endl;
+        }
+
         // check for bad triangle (without tolerance)
         if (dist < radius1) bad[t] = true;
       }
